# Every variable deploy/docker-compose.yml reads, grouped by the service that reads it.
# Copy to `.env` (same folder) and edit before `docker compose up`. Every key is optional.
#
# How to read it:
#   An active line, `KEY=value`: set on purpose; copying this file sets it.
#   A commented line, `# KEY=value`: the COMPOSE DEFAULT, shown but not set. Copying the file
#     changes nothing, and a later change to that default in compose still reaches you, which a
#     pinned copy here would silently block. Uncomment to override.
#   SECRET in the comment above a key: always EMPTY in this file. Fill it in only in your own
#     deploy/.env, which is gitignored for exactly that reason.
#
# A key that is present but EMPTY counts as unset for every ${VAR:-default} in compose, so
# `KEY=` falls through to the default rather than passing an empty string.
#
# No service loads this file whole: each one receives only the keys compose lists for it, and
# tools/tests/test_compose_env_scope.py fails if any service is handed the whole file through
# `env_file`, every other rail's secrets included.
#
# tools/tests/test_env_example.py fails when compose reads a variable this file does not name,
# commented or not, so an operator following this file never starts with a key unset and nothing
# saying so.

# =============================================================================================
# Shared by most services
# =============================================================================================

# The rails this deployment serves, comma-separated. Leave it out (or blank) for the gateway's
# default, which is every rail; set it only to run a subset. To drop one or two rails, prefer
# PLATFORM_DISABLED_APPS below, which does not have to be edited when a new rail ships. Takes
# effect on the next `docker compose up` of the gateway. Until 2026-10 the full stack did not pass
# this to the gateway at all, so a value here was silently ignored.
# PLATFORM_ENABLED_APPS=

# Rails THIS box deliberately does not run, comma-separated (e.g. `meeting-atlas,co-worker`, or a
# rail whose corpus lives on another machine). Subtracted from the gateway's enabled set; blank
# disables nothing. The gateway reads it at start, so it takes effect on the next
# `docker compose up` of the gateway. The maintainers' smoke sweep (tools/rail_smoke.py, not in
# the public snapshot) reads this same line and prints each rail named here as SKIP instead of
# probing it, so the sweep and the gateway agree on what this box runs.
# The ops scripts read the same line: rebuild-stale.ps1 leaves these rails out of its plan, and
# deploy/installer/sync-disabled-rails.ps1 (which rebuild-stale and platform-startup also run)
# stops their containers and sets restart=no, so the engine does not bring them back at boot.
# Taking a rail out of this line and running that script again restores restart=always and starts it.
# PLATFORM_DISABLED_APPS=

# The container runtime THIS box runs the stack on: podman, desktop (Docker Desktop) or wsl. Not
# read by compose. The ops scripts (platform-startup, smoke-test, apply, preflight,
# activate-model-roles) read it FIRST and only infer the runtime (which CLI lists
# platform-gateway-1) when it is absent; an explicit -RuntimeMode on a script still beats it. If
# another runtime lists the gateway running, they say so in a warning naming both and still follow
# this line. The installer and apply.ps1 write it for the runtime they set up. Commented here, so
# a copy of this file declares nothing.
# PLATFORM_RUNTIME=

# Local display timezone. Recordings and harvested items carry UTC timestamps; every rail that
# shows a time converts with this. A wrong value shifts everything by hours while looking
# entirely plausible, which is the worst way for a clock to be wrong.
TZ=America/Los_Angeles

# The address `host.docker.internal` resolves to inside every container, i.e. where the NATIVE
# broker (port 11500) is reached. `host-gateway` is Docker's own special value for the host;
# set an IP only if your runtime does not support it.
# WINDOWS_HOST=host-gateway

# --- Broker control plane ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECRET. One platform-wide shared secret. Compose passes it to every broker client; the broker
# enforces it ONLY when set, so leaving it empty keeps a dev box open. Set it and the broker
# stops accepting unauthenticated /v1/* calls from anything on the LAN or in any container.
#
# It has to be set in TWO places, and clients FIRST or you get a 401 gap: here (compose picks it
# up on the next `up`), then on the broker's own service environment, then restart the broker.
BROKER_AUTH_TOKEN=

# The same secret delivered as a FILE PATH instead of a value. Supported by every broker client
# and by the broker itself; when both are set, the FILE wins.
#
# Why you would want it: an environment variable is visible in `docker inspect`, in a crash dump,
# and in /proc/<pid>/environ inside the container, so anything that can read a backend's
# environment (an in-container shell escape, for one) can read this token, and it is the single
# credential every broker client shares.
#
# NOT WIRED UP YET, deliberately: compose does not read this key today. Switching over is a
# compose change (a mount or a docker secret, plus BROKER_AUTH_TOKEN_FILE on each service) and
# wants reviewing on its own; the capability shipped first so the switch is a config edit rather
# than a code change. Leave it blank to keep using the value above; that path is unchanged.
#
# Contents are read whole and stripped. A path set here that cannot be read makes each RAIL send
# no token (visible: the broker 401s) and makes the BROKER refuse to start (deliberate: an empty
# token there means the control plane is open, which has no symptom at all).
BROKER_AUTH_TOKEN_FILE=

# --- Client timeouts (each service's *_BROKER_TIMEOUT, and BROKER_MEDIA_TIMEOUT where set) -----
# Seconds a rail waits on one broker call. Each must sit ABOVE the broker's own media_timeout
# (1200 s), or the client gives up first: the worker keeps running, the retry queues behind the
# job still holding the GPU, and the broker's own 502 never reaches the caller. 1320 is the
# broker's 1200 plus headroom, so a timeout here means something is genuinely wedged.

# --- Model slots (each service's *_MODEL keys, and *_ROLE where a service has one) -------------
# Defaults are broker @roles, resolved through services/broker/roles.json and repointable live
# in Admin > Rails. Putting a concrete model name here pins it and silently wins over whatever
# the panel says, so override only when you mean to take the rail out of the panel's control.

# =============================================================================================
# gateway (the shell, login and reverse proxy)
# =============================================================================================

# Initial admin, seeded on FIRST run only (when the auth DB is empty).
# SECRET (the password). If PLATFORM_ADMIN_PASSWORD is left blank, the gateway generates a strong
# random password and prints it ONCE to its log:  docker compose logs gateway
PLATFORM_ADMIN_USER=admin
PLATFORM_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Session-cookie hardening. Set to true the moment you serve over HTTPS / expose the platform
# publicly (see INSTRUCTIONS.md "HTTPS: when it matters"). Plain-HTTP requests to a loopback host
# (localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1) still get a non-Secure cookie either way, so local scripts and the
# smoke test keep working over http://localhost:1111.
PLATFORM_COOKIE_SECURE=false

# Gateway -> broker ceiling (see "Client timeouts" above).
# PLATFORM_BROKER_TIMEOUT=1320

# How long a login lasts, in hours (7 days by default). Shorter is safer on a shared machine.
# PLATFORM_SESSION_TTL_HOURS=168

# The nightly model-pool scan writes a short description of each newly seen model with this
# broker role or model, at most PLATFORM_DESCRIBE_CAP models per run.
# PLATFORM_DESCRIBE_MODEL=@chat-fast
# PLATFORM_DESCRIBE_CAP=8

# The largest request body, in bytes, the gateway relays to a rail whose rail.json declares no
# `max_request_bytes` of its own; a larger one is refused with a 413 (docs/RAIL_CONTRACT.md,
# "Request bodies"). Default 27262976 (26 MiB: one 25 MiB file plus its form).
# PLATFORM_MAX_REQUEST_BYTES=27262976

# The Origins the WebSocket proxy (a rail's terminal or live view) accepts a handshake from. Unset
# or blank, the browser's Origin must be the address the platform was reached on (same-origin),
# which is right for one front door. Behind more than one name, list each as the browser sends it
# (scheme://host[:port]), comma-separated; a JSON array works too.
# PLATFORM_ALLOWED_WS_ORIGINS=https://platform.example.com,http://localhost:1111

# =============================================================================================
# recipe-book
# =============================================================================================

# Broker ceiling (see "Client timeouts" above).
# RECIPE_BOOK_BROKER_TIMEOUT=1320

# Model slots (see "Model slots" above): the assistant, the recipe-photo reader, the
# per-recipe icon IMAGE model (media worker, not Ollama), and semantic search's embedder.
# RECIPE_BOOK_ASSISTANT_MODEL=@recipe
# RECIPE_BOOK_VISION_MODEL=@recipe-vision
# RECIPE_BOOK_ICON_MODEL=@recipe-icon
# RECIPE_BOOK_EMBED_MODEL=@embed

# The platform user who inherits the data from before per-user scoping (meal plan, pantry, bar).
# Set it to whichever account should own that data on your deployment.
# RECIPE_BOOK_PRIMARY_USER=admin

# "Send to Phone" (per-user Google Tasks). Set the shared OAuth APP credentials and redirect URI,
# and each user connects their own Google account in-app. Blank = feature off (no Connect
# button). One-time setup: rails/recipe-book/docs/GOOGLE_TASKS.md.
# SECRET: the client secret.
RECIPE_BOOK_GTASKS_CLIENT_ID=
RECIPE_BOOK_GTASKS_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Must match the web client's registered redirect URI, e.g.
#   https://platform.example.com/recipe-book/api/gtasks/callback
RECIPE_BOOK_GTASKS_REDIRECT_URI=
# RECIPE_BOOK_GTASKS_LIST_TITLE=Shopping List

# SECRET. Fernet key that encrypts stored Google refresh tokens at rest. Blank = stored in
# plaintext (legacy). Rotating it invalidates every stored token, so users re-connect.
# Generate one:  python -c "from cryptography.fernet import Fernet; print(Fernet.generate_key().decode())"
RECIPE_BOOK_TOKEN_KEY=

# =============================================================================================
# workstation (a browser terminal: an SSH client into the host. Highest-privilege rail.)
# =============================================================================================

# The SSH target as seen from inside the container, and the account to log in as there. Needs
# WORKSTATION_KEYS_HOST to hold `id_workstation` + `known_hosts`. See rails/workstation/README.md.
# WORKSTATION_SSH_HOST=host.docker.internal
# WORKSTATION_SSH_PORT=22
WORKSTATION_SSH_USER=

# Host folder mounted read-only at /keys (the private key and known_hosts). Relative paths are
# resolved from this deploy/ folder.
# WORKSTATION_KEYS_HOST=./workstation-keys

# First-run convenience ONLY: true accepts any host key, which is exactly what an SSH
# man-in-the-middle needs. Populate known_hosts and leave this false.
# WORKSTATION_INSECURE_SKIP_HOST_KEY_CHECK=false

# Folder each terminal preset starts in, as a path ON THE SSH TARGET. Empty = wherever the
# target's login shell lands. Also the folder the published VS Code RemoteApp opens.
WORKSTATION_START_DIR=

# RemoteApp (published desktop apps). The RDP host is the address the CLIENT dials; this backend
# never speaks RDP, it only hands out an .rdp launcher. Empty host = feature off. Publish the app
# on the host first: deploy/install-remoteapp.ps1.
WORKSTATION_RDP_HOST=
# WORKSTATION_RDP_PORT=3389
WORKSTATION_RDP_USERNAME=

# =============================================================================================
# terminal-fun
# =============================================================================================

# Model slot for the in-game assistant (see "Model slots" above).
# TERMINAL_FUN_LLM_MODEL=@terminal-fun

# =============================================================================================
# ai-playground
# =============================================================================================

# Model slots (see "Model slots" above): local generation and the retrieval embedder.
# AI_PLAYGROUND_CHAT_MODEL=@ai-playground
# AI_PLAYGROUND_EMBED_MODEL=@embed

# SECRET. NVIDIA NIM key for the in-demo cloud toggle. Empty = the toggle is greyed out.
AI_PLAYGROUND_NVIDIA_API_KEY=
# AI_PLAYGROUND_NVIDIA_BASE_URL=https://integrate.api.nvidia.com/v1
# A plain-instruct model, verified by a real completion. A listing in /v1/models is not proof
# your key is entitled to it; see the rail's nim.py.
# AI_PLAYGROUND_NVIDIA_CHAT_MODEL=meta/llama-3.2-11b-vision-instruct
# Host Ollama the Embedding Lab pulls new embedders through (its pull button and the weekly
# "Embedding model update"). Must be reachable FROM the container; a run whose pulls all fail
# now shows as an error on Admin > Schedule rather than "ok".
# AI_PLAYGROUND_OLLAMA_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434
# Blank (the default): that weekly update SKIPS the model @embed resolves to (bge-m3 today), and
# its run record says so. That model made every rail's stored vectors, and a new upstream digest
# would change the weights under all of them with nothing re-embedding. Set 1 only alongside a
# planned re-index of every RAG rail.
# AI_PLAYGROUND_REPULL_EMBEDDER=

# =============================================================================================
# co-worker
# =============================================================================================

# Model slot for the executive brief (see "Model slots" above).
# CO_WORKER_SYNTHESIS_MODEL=@co-worker-synthesis

# The inbox owner's display name and address. Items whose `from` contains either are relabelled
# "(self)" before synthesis, so the brief never says "Reply to <yourself>". Blank turns the
# filter off. This file is tracked: put the real values in deploy/.env only.
CO_WORKER_USER_NAME=
CO_WORKER_USER_EMAIL=

# The PLATFORM USERNAME the harvested inbox belongs to (the login name, not the display name
# above). A synthesis pass carries a requester to the broker, and so may be answered from that
# person's own Claude CLI subscription, only when this exact user asked for it; anyone else's
# refresh, and every refresh while this is blank, runs on the local model. Whether the role
# reaches the CLI at all is still the broker's decision (BROKER_CLI_* and the Rails switch).
# Names only here: put the real value in deploy/.env.
CO_WORKER_INBOX_OWNER=

# Re-synthesize automatically when the harvested inbox changed since the last brief (true/false),
# and the minimum gap in seconds between two automatic passes. A manual click ignores the gap.
# CO_WORKER_AUTO_SYNTHESIZE=true
# CO_WORKER_AUTO_SYNTHESIZE_MIN_INTERVAL_S=900

# Both mounts are OPTIONAL. Left blank, compose falls back to an empty named volume, which yields
# an empty dashboard / hidden prompts panel rather than a startup failure.
#
# Docker Desktop on Windows uses forward slashes with a drive letter:
#   CO_WORKER_INBOX_MOUNT=D:/data/co-worker/inbox
# WSL-style paths also work:
#   CO_WORKER_INBOX_MOUNT=/d/data/co-worker/inbox

# Where the harvest loops drop JSON items: the same directory the scheduled co-work tasks
# write to.
CO_WORKER_INBOX_MOUNT=

# The scheduler's job directory, mounted READ-ONLY. One subdir per job, each with a SKILL.md.
# Powers the "Co-Work Prompts" panel in the dashboard. Blank hides the panel.
#   e.g. CO_WORKER_PROMPTS_MOUNT=C:/Users/you/Claude/Scheduled
CO_WORKER_PROMPTS_MOUNT=

# =============================================================================================
# smb-partner-enablement
# =============================================================================================

# Broker ceiling (see "Client timeouts" above).
# SMB_PARTNER_BROKER_TIMEOUT=1320

# Model slots (see "Model slots" above).
# SMB_PARTNER_RAG_MODEL=@smb-partner-rag
# SMB_PARTNER_EMBED_MODEL=@embed

# 1 = load the RAG model and embedder at container start. OFF by default: nothing at boot needs
# them, and on a 24 GB card the RAG role resolves to a 24B model that would sit on the card for
# 30 minutes after every container start whether or not anyone opened the rail.
SMB_PARTNER_WARM_ON_BOOT=

# auto | broker | browser | off. `auto` probes the broker's media worker and falls back to the
# browser's own speech synthesis.
# SMB_PARTNER_VOICE_BACKEND=auto

# =============================================================================================
# gemini-cx
# =============================================================================================

# Broker ceiling (see "Client timeouts" above).
# GEMINI_CX_BROKER_TIMEOUT=1320

# Model slots (see "Model slots" above).
# GEMINI_CX_RAG_MODEL=@gemini-cx-rag
# GEMINI_CX_EMBED_MODEL=@embed

# 1 = warm the model pair at container start. OFF by default, for the same reason as
# SMB_PARTNER_WARM_ON_BOOT above.
GEMINI_CX_WARM_ON_BOOT=

# Read-aloud: auto | broker | browser | off, and the Kokoro speaker the broker path uses.
# GEMINI_CX_VOICE_BACKEND=auto
# GEMINI_CX_VOICE_SPEAKER=af_heart

# =============================================================================================
# meeting-atlas
# =============================================================================================

# Both mounts are OPTIONAL and both are read-only; the rail never writes to either. Left blank,
# compose falls back to empty named volumes, which yields an empty rail rather than a crash.

# The Meetily recordings tree.
MEETING_ATLAS_RECORDINGS_MOUNT=

# The Meetily SQLite: the only place the real meeting title and Meetily's own summary live.
# ⚠ This is the database FILE, not the directory holding it: it mounts at /data/meetily.db and
# MEETING_ATLAS_MEETILY_DB is derived from it. There used to be a second variable naming the
# in-container path; it is gone, and an .env still carrying the old DIRECTORY form here will bind
# a directory onto a file path. Without this, titles come from summary.json sidecars instead.
#   e.g. MEETING_ATLAS_MEETILY_DB_MOUNT=C:/Users/you/AppData/Roaming/com.meetily.ai/meeting_minutes.sqlite
MEETING_ATLAS_MEETILY_DB_MOUNT=

# Seconds between checks for changed recordings; a rebuild happens only when the mount's
# fingerprint changed. 0 = rebuild only on POST /api/reindex.
# MEETING_ATLAS_AUTOREINDEX_SECONDS=300

# false = the audio route answers 404, so recordings cannot be played from the rail.
# MEETING_ATLAS_SERVE_AUDIO=true

# =============================================================================================
# openmaic (the rail) and openmaic-app (the upstream courseware app it fronts)
# =============================================================================================

# Broker ceiling (see "Client timeouts" above).
# OPENMAIC_BROKER_TIMEOUT=1320
# The rail's reverse-proxy wait on openmaic-app, whose generating requests wait on that same
# broker call; kept at the ceiling so the proxy never cuts a CLI call's local fallback.
# OPENMAIC_APP_TIMEOUT=1320

# Model slots (see "Model slots" above).
# OPENMAIC_LLM_MODEL=@openmaic
# OPENMAIC_EMBED_MODEL=@embed

# SINGLE-USER BOXES ONLY. The PLATFORM USERNAME (the login name) every generation the app makes
# is attributed to. The app calls the rail's shim with the service token and no platform
# identity, so nothing on a call says who is waiting on it; this says "it is always this
# person". Set, the shim names this account as `requester` on the app's calls on the @openmaic
# role while it is the ONLY user the rail has seen in the app for the last 30 minutes, and so
# they may be answered from that person's own Claude CLI subscription; anyone else in the app,
# and it names nobody. Blank: NO call carries a requester, so openmaic never reaches the Claude
# CLI, whatever the Rails switch says; setting this is the one step on openmaic's side that lets
# @openmaic reach it. Whether the role reaches the CLI is then still the broker's decision
# (BROKER_CLI_* and the Rails switch). Needs OPENMAIC_SHIM_TOKEN below set to this box's own
# secret: with the published default any container can call the shim as the app does, so the
# shim names nobody (and says why, once, in the rail's log). Names only here: put the real value
# in deploy/.env.
OPENMAIC_CLI_REQUESTER=

# SECRET. Shared secret the rail's OpenAI-compatible shim requires on /api/llm/v1; openmaic-app
# presents it. Empty = the compose dev default, `openmaic-local-dev`, which anyone who has read
# this repo knows. Set a random value.
OPENMAIC_SHIM_TOKEN=

# Redirect generation to an external OpenAI-compatible endpoint (a larger-VRAM host on the LAN,
# a hosted provider) instead of the shim. Empty = the shim, so every token goes through the
# broker's GPU gate. Compose substitutes it into openmaic-app too; the rail reads it only to
# label its reasoning chip. It must be outside this stack: openmaic-app's own network reaches the
# rail and nothing else of it.
OPENMAIC_LLM_BASE_URL=

# SECRET. The credential for that external endpoint. Empty = openmaic-app presents
# OPENMAIC_SHIM_TOKEN, which is right only while generation goes to the shim.
OPENMAIC_LLM_API_KEY=

# openmaic-app is a PRE-BUILT image (its source is not in this repo). Name the tag you built or
# pulled.
# OPENMAIC_APP_IMAGE=openmaic-app:latest

# MANDATORY in the app, which has no model fallback of its own. The `ollama:` prefix is required
# too: without it the string is parsed as an OpenAI model and never reaches the shim.
# OPENMAIC_DEFAULT_MODEL=ollama:@openmaic

# =============================================================================================
# watch-builder
# =============================================================================================

# Model slot (see "Model slots" above): the designer that turns a description into a watch
# face spec.
# WATCH_BUILDER_DESIGNER_MODEL=@watch-builder-designer
